CORE smt-backend no-new-smt
main.c
--smt2
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ u >= 2\^53: SUCCESS$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Regression test for float-to-integer conversion in the SMT2 bit-blasting
back-end (float_bvt::to_integer in src/solvers/floatbv/float_bv.cpp).

CBMC has two encodings for floating-point: bit-blasting (the default, and
also used by --smt2 without --fpa) and FP theory (--fpa, --cvc5,
--bitwuzla).  The bug was in the bit-blasting path only: the unpacked
fraction was not padded to dest_width before right-shifting, so for values
with exponent > spec.f (i.e. doubles >= 2^53) the shift distance
underflowed and the result was zero.

Every double in [2^53, 2^54) is an integer >= 2^53, so the assertion holds
for every concrete input, yet the unpatched --smt2 back-end reported
VERIFICATION FAILED.  See main.c for a bit-level walkthrough, and
test_sat.desc for the SAT/boolbv control, which already did the padding
correctly.
